## Find something to do

Open **My work** to see items assigned to you. To see a team's queue, choose its project and open **Work**. Expand a status group and select a title for the description and discussion. Use **Filters** to narrow a long list.

## Try a small handoff

In a project where you can edit, create a Work item such as \u201CCheck the visitor guide.\u201D Include what to check, who should do it and how to tell that it is finished. A comment records a conversation. Set the status after the outcome is ready.

## Read or edit a page

Open **Docs**, choose a collection and select a page title. **Read** shows the saved Markdown. **Edit** lets an authorized member change the body, **Preview** shows the formatting and **Source** exposes the full Markdown. Save at Home, then reopen the page to confirm the revision. A private draft stays private until someone explicitly publishes it.

## Find it again

Use workspace **Search** for a phrase from the page or Work item. Press Enter to search and open an authorized result. The browser's Back button returns you to your previous view.
`;var Zb=`# Settings and access

## Make the workspace yours

Open **Settings** in the workspace sidebar. Choose **System**, **Light** or **Dark** for Appearance. System follows your device. On a narrow screen, use **Browse workspace** to open the sidebar.

**Show Stead Handbook in Projects** controls the Handbook entry for your account in this browser. Turn it off to shorten your project list. Return to Settings and turn it on to restore the entry. A saved Handbook link still opens while the entry is hidden.

## What other people see

Changing appearance or hiding the Handbook affects your view. Your teammates keep their own preferences. Other projects show only when your Home has granted you access. If you expected a project, ask its maintainer to check which Urbit identity has membership.

## Sign in on the private pilot

Enter your Urbit ID on the Home sign-in page. Open your own identity ship's approval page, compare the code and the Home address, and approve the matching request. Return to Stead and check the approval. Signing out ends that browser session.
`;var Kb=`# Give an agent a task

Start with a Work item, a specific deliverable and a person who can judge the result. A document edit is a good first task. An agent can help in a project that has no code repository.

## Write a brief someone can review

For example, \u201CMove the opening meeting from Monday to Wednesday in the visitor guide. Keep the time, meeting point and all other wording. Show the proposed change for review.\u201D

Link or identify the exact page, describe what the agent may change, and say when it should ask a question. For this example, it should ask if the page contains two different Monday meetings.

## Choose the worker

The Owner is the person accountable for the outcome. On a Home with registered agent workers, **Working on it** identifies the worker. The current private pilot shows **Assignee**. Assigning a name or copying the brief does not start a model. If your Home has no connected agent runner, the brief is still usable for a manual handoff.

## Does each agent need a moon?

No. A moon gives a worker its own ship address under a parent planet. Use one when the worker needs to communicate as its own Urbit ship. A Home can also identify a registered agent and bind it to a worker runtime. The operator connects that runtime to a local model or a compatible hosted provider. Neither a moon's parent planet nor a model name grants access to a project. [How Urbit IDs work](https://docs.urbit.org/urbit-id/what-is-urbit-id)

Grant the worker only the project, document and actions this task requires. Keep a human owner and choose a reviewer. Never share a person's ship sign-in code with a model.

## Review the result

Ask for the exact proposed edit. Compare it with the original and request a revision if anything else changed. The reviewer checks the current document and accepts the exact change. Mark the Work done after you can reopen the accepted result.

The complete assign, clarify, revise and accept journey is still being tested. A model response or a comment alone does not change the saved document.
`;var Jb=`# Common questions

## Where is Code?

Look beside **Docs** in a project that has a repository you can access. General projects can use Work and Docs on their own. The Handbook's **Code** tab has a Git README you can explore.

## Can I try this without changing team work?

Yes. Open the example card in the Handbook's **Work** tab, explore the guides under **Docs**, and switch between **Read** and **Raw** in **Code**. These examples only contain material shipped with Stead.

## Can I hide this project and still read an article?

Yes. **Settings** controls whether the Handbook appears in your sidebar. Keep a bookmark to any article, or restore the project from Settings.

## How do I know a change was saved?

A draft or proposed edit still needs acceptance. After saving, reopen the Work item or page and check the saved content and revision. For code, check the commit displayed by the project.

## How does the Handbook stay current?

The guides travel with each Stead release. A maintainer can change the Markdown alongside the feature it explains. Everyone running that release reads the same version, including someone who hid the Handbook and opens it again later.
`;var Yp=`# Working with Git in Stead

Open a software project and choose **Code** beside **Docs**. The file list shows the snapshot that Home has accepted. Use the branch or tag picker to choose a version. Open a folder or file. For a Markdown file, **Read** shows the formatted page and **Raw** shows its exact text.

## Clone a project

1. In the project's **Code** view, expand **Clone**. Copy the **Clone URL**.
2. Choose **Create read credential** if your Home offers it. Copy the credential while it is visible and note when it expires.
3. In a terminal, run the command below with your copied URL in place of \`CLONE_URL\`. When Git asks, enter \`stead\` as the username and paste the Git credential at the password prompt.

\`\`\`sh
git clone CLONE_URL my-project
cd my-project
git log -1 --oneline
\`\`\`

The characters you paste at the password prompt may stay invisible. Keep the credential out of URLs, shell commands, issues and screenshots. Your Urbit sign-in approves your browser session. The Git credential gives your Git client access to this repository.

## Check which version you have

In your clone, fetch updated references and inspect the latest commit on the remote branch. Substitute your branch name if the project uses a different one.

\`\`\`sh
git fetch origin
git log -1 --oneline origin/main
\`\`\`

Compare that commit with the one shown in **Code** for the same branch. Fetching leaves your working files alone. A branch can move when a new change is accepted. A commit ID identifies one exact version.

## Share a change

A read credential covers clone and fetch. You can still edit files and commit in your local clone. Ask the project maintainer which remote and review process accept contributions before pushing. The private pilot has verified clone and fetch. An authorized review and merge can be claimed only when the Home confirms them.

## If Git stops working

For an authentication error, check the username, the credential's expiry and your current project access. Your Git client may have cached an older password for this repository. For a connection error on the private pilot, check the private connection first. A project shows **Code** when it has a repository you can access.

This Handbook README is a reading example. Get the clone URL and credential from the project whose files you want.
